---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclosure from '../components/Disclosure.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Jane.com | Chris Dabel"
  description="The Airflow and Spark pipeline that built training data for a boutique marketplace's recommendation model from a billion clickstream rows, and the data-quality DAGs that guarded it."
>
  <h1>The pipeline behind a recommendation model</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Senior Data Engineer II at Jane.com, a boutique e-commerce marketplace,
    February 2020 to January 2022. Sole data engineer for four months after
    turnover.
  </p>
  <Disclosure />

  <section aria-labelledby="problem">
    <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      The marketplace's product recommendations ran on a model the data
      science team retrained on shopper behavior. Training it meant turning
      raw clickstream into a feature set at a scale the existing SQL Server
      Integration Services jobs could not reach, on a cadence that let the
      team iterate, with predictions landing somewhere analysts could read
      them the same hour.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="pipeline">
    <h2 id="pipeline">The pipeline</h2>
    <dl class="steps">
      <div>
        <dt>Training data on EMR</dt>
        <dd>
          Airflow DAGs ran PySpark and SparkSQL on AWS EMR to build the
          training set from a billion clickstream rows over a rolling
          ten-month lookback. The window moved with each run, so the model
          always trained on the most recent ten months of behavior and never
          on a frozen snapshot.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Post-processing through SageMaker</dt>
        <dd>
          Model output came back through SageMaker for post-processing in
          the same DAG family, so training and scoring shared one
          orchestration path and one set of alerts.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Predictions to Redshift, hourly</dt>
        <dd>
          A Python DAG wrote the model's prediction outputs to Redshift
          tables every hour, which gave analysts current data for trend
          analysis without asking the data science team for an export.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Data-quality DAGs in front of the model</dt>
        <dd>
          Separate DAGs scanned for anomalies in the inputs, logged what they
          found, and alerted, so a bad day of clickstream was caught before
          it trained the model that chose what shoppers saw. The resume line
          says it plainly: this improved the recommendations.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="around">
    <h2 id="around">Around it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Two critical SSIS workbooks migrated into Python pipelines in
        Airflow, and about a dozen more DAGs built alongside them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Airflow subject-matter expert for the team: runbooks, data
        definitions, and training that raised adoption and shortened
        onboarding. Mentored two junior engineers.
      </li>
      <li>
        Advised the incoming manager on modernizing the platform, including
        AWS integration strategy and adopting dbt, which set the roadmap
        after the turnover.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="stack">
    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Apache Airflow, PySpark and SparkSQL on AWS EMR, SageMaker, Redshift,
      Python, and the SSIS estate being retired.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.4; margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  .steps { margin: 0; }
  .steps div { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .steps dt { font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  .steps dd { margin: 0; }
  ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
</style>
